        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The purpose of this experiment was to determine how various aspects of pulmonary function were affected by weightlessness. It is known that gravity plays a major role in determining the distribution of ventilation, blood flow, gas exchange, alveolar size, intrapleural pressures, and mechanical stresses within the human lung.  Thus, marked changes in pulmonary function during weightlessness are predictable. The measurements for this experiment were made using a battery of non-invasive breathing tests. Preflight and postflight the test sequence was performed in both the supine and erect positions, enabling a comparison of the effects of recumbency, with the effects of microgravity. Repetition of the inflight tests at intervals during the mission and at intervals during the week immediately postflight, allowed for adaptive changes to be studied.
<P>
The primary experimental objectives were to determine the following parameters before, 
during and after spaceflight:
<P><ul>
a. distribution of ventilation/gas mixing in the lung<br>

b. distribution of perfusion<br>

c. distribution of ventilation/perfusion ratios<br>

d. pulmonary diffusing capacity<br>

e. pulmonary capillary blood volume<br>

f. overall lung mechanics<br>

g. overall pulmonary gas exchange<br>

h. total pulmonary blood flow<P></ul>
<P>

APPROACH:<BR>

Data were acquired in the following sessions:<P><ul>

<u>Preflight</u>:  Pulmonary Function Test, Extended Pulmonary Function Test, Head-Down Tilt Simulation Study br>

<u><P>Inflight</u>:  Pulmonary Function Test<br>

<u><P>Postflight</u>:  Pulmonary Function Test<p></ul>

RESULTS:<BR>
Cardiac output and stroke volume were raised by exposure to microgravity, and remained elevated through the first four days of flight with a suggestion that they were still raised at the end of the flight.  Diffusing capacity was elevated in microgravity, and the lack of adaptive changes over the course of the flight suggests that this change is directly due to removal of the gravitational force on the lung.  The subdivisions of diffusing capacity, pulmonary capillary blood volume and membrane diffusing capacity both rose significantly in microgravity, and suggest that the lung is much more uniformly perfused with blood in microgravity.
<P>
Hyperventilation-breath-hold studies of the distribution of pulmonary perfusion in microgravity suggest that the lung is much more uniformly perfused, consistent with the changes seen in the diffusing capacity measurements. However, the persistence of cardiogenic oscillations in expired CO2 shows that there is an unexplored residual inhomogeneity of pulmonary perfusion that is intrinsic to the lung.
<P>
There is a similar reduction in the scale of ventilatory inhomogeneity measured during vital capacity techniques. Cardiogenic oscillations and phase IV height were reduced in microgravity, but closing volume remained unaltered. As was the case with pulmonary perfusion, considerable inhomogeneity of pulmonary ventilation persists in microgravity. Multiple breath N2 washouts showed no change in microgravity, and show that the inhomogeneity of ventilation seen in normal subjects during tidal breathing is primarily non-gravitational in origin and intrinsic to the lung, a surprising finding.
<P>
There are also changes in lung volumes in microgravity. Functional residual capacity is reduced, 
but there is no decrease in vital capacity except early in flight. There is a surprising reduction 
in residual volume that is most likely caused by the reduction in gravitational deformation of the 
lung in microgravity. A decrease in the resting tidal volume suggests that there are changes in the 
neurological control of breathing. At present, many of these changes are poorly understood, and 
further analysis of existing data as well as new measurements are planned.<p>
        </div>

